        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="false" referenceHouse="false" greenerHomesTab="true">
                <Vermiculite code="3">
                    <English>No Vermiculite</English>
                    <French>Pas de Vermiculite</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>A $5,000 Good Energy air sealing rebate is available if you reduce air leakage from 3 or more air changes to 1.5 air changes or less (@50Pa). This can be achieved through a deep energy retrofit with comprehensive air sealing details performed on walls, ceilings and all building penetrations. Houses with low levels of air leakage require a balanced ventilation system, such as a heat recovery ventilator (HRV), to provide fresh air to the home and to exhaust stale air. See ventilation system comments for Good Energy HRV rebates.



An additional $500 to $1,000 grant is available through Canada Greener Homes if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed in this report. Achieving the target defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.



</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>Up to $12,000 in a Good Energy insulation rebate for above-grade walls is available if you add at minimum one times the existing wall insulation to the exterior of all above-grade walls. Consider adding two times the existing wall insulation to exterior walls for a more robust wall system that reduces the potential for moisture condensation. Adding two times the existing wall insulation can be an effective strategy for 2x4 walls but may not be as feasible or cost-effective for 2x6 or deeper wall systems.  

An additional $5,000 is available through Canada Greener Homes. Incentives are based on the percentage of wall area to which the insulation has been added and the amount of insulation added. A minimum of 20% of the exterior wall area of your home, excluding foundation walls, must be insulated in order to qualify for the grant. 

</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>ATTIC / CEILING INSULATION 

Up to $7,500 in incentives is available through Canada Greener Homes grants and Good Energy rebates for increasing insulation in attics, cathedral ceilings and flat roofs. The amount you are eligible for will depend on factors such as what insulation is currently in your home, how much insulation you add and the type of attic/ceiling your home has. If your house consists of more than one roof or roof type, the grant amount will be pro-rated or calculated based on roof type and area. 



ROOFING MEMBRANE

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $150 is available for installing a self-adhering roofing underlayment applied to the entire roof surface. This resiliency measure must be combined with an energy efficiency retrofit from the initiative to be eligible for a grant. 



</ceilings>
                <ceilingsCathedral>The insulation in your cathedral ceiling was assessed at R-20. Because the amount of insulation in your cathedral ceiling has been assessed as being greater than R-12 but less than or equal to R-25, a grant of up to $750 is available for adding insulation to reach R-40 or more. 

</ceilingsCathedral>
                <foundation>Up to $10,000 in a Good Energy insulation rebate for below-grade walls is available if you add R20 or greater to all below-grade walls. The insulation in the below-grade wall system must be R20 or less prior to the retrofit. The rebate is proportional to the depth of the wall below grade. Walls that are 6 feet below-grade or deeper are eligible for the full rebate amount. A wall that is 3 feet below-grade is eligible for 50% of the rebate. 



An additional $1,500 is available through Canada Greener Homes for adding insulation to basement walls. Grants are based on the R-value of the added insulation and the percentage of wall area insulated. The current amount of insulation does not affect the Canada Greener Homes grant amounts. A minimum of 20% of the exterior foundation wall area must be insulated. 



Basement - Waterproofing

A grant of $875 is available for waterproofing exterior below-grade basement walls. This resiliency measure must be combined with an energy efficiency retrofit from the initiative to be eligible for a grant. 



Basement Header

A grant of $240 is available through Canada Greener Homes for sealing and adding insulation to basement headers. In an unfinished basement, sealing and insulating headers can be a relatively simple task. The grant of $240 is available when R-20 or more is added to a minimum of 80% of your entire basement header area, along with air sealing improvements. 

</foundation>
                <AdditionalComments>Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, homeowners are eligible for reimbursement of up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and a grant of up to $5,000 in total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed on or after December 1, 2020. 



Hiring a contractor is required for several of the home retrofits and strongly recommended for the others. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines. 



NRCan does not endorse the services of any contractor, nor any specific product, and accepts no liability in the selection of materials, products, contractors or performance of workmanship. Before undertaking retrofits, find out about the appropriate products, safety and installation techniques, and ensure that all retrofits meet local building codes and by-laws. 



ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION 

For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant. If you have limited access to the internet, please consult the Homeowner Information Kit that was mailed to you. 



Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative have certain conditions for eligibility. These include: 

You must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report 

If you wish to receive a grant for undertaking resiliency measures or installing a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify 

Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the Searchable product list of eligible equipment for the Canada Greener Homes Grant 

All mechanical systems (heat pumps, heat pump water heaters, furnaces and boilers) must be installed by a licensed and trained professional 

It is highly recommended that you obtain attestations (if applicable for your retrofits) confirming that the installation of a mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional, the ground source heat pump system was installed in accordance with CSA standards, and/or the air source heat pump or cold climate air source heat pump system is capable of distributing heat throughout your entire home 

All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada. Used equipment and associated installation costs are not eligible for a grant. 





ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR FUNDING 

You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure. 



Participants will be required to declare funding or rebates received from other sources through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal. 



Programs in your region that you may be eligible for include: 

Good Energy rebates Â https://yukon.ca/en/buildings-and-homes

Better Buildings Program Â https://yukon.ca/en/housing-and-property/home-energy-rebates/get-funding-complete-residential-or-commercial-building

 Canada Greener Homes Loan Program - https://natural-resources.canada.ca/energy-efficiency/homes/canada-greener-homes-initiative/canada-greener-homes-loan/24286

Yukon Housing Home Repair program Â https://yukon.ca/en/housing-and-property/funding-and-loans/apply-loan-repair-your-home</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
            <GreenerHomes basementSlabInsulated="true" moistureProofCrawlSpace="false" waterproofing="false" roofingMembrane="false" minR10ContinuousExposedFloors="false" backwaterValve="false" sumpPump="false" smartThermostats="true" remoteCommunities="true" evaluationCost="50">
                <Community>Teslin; Teslin Post 13</Community>
            </GreenerHomes>
        </Options>
